CLAIM 18: Resume / crash recovery

GOAL: Verify stale in-progress work can be reconciled and a run can resume from persisted state.

== STATIC CODE EVIDENCE ==

Persistence (the substrate resume reads from):
- src/goal/run-store.ts:15-18 `GOALS_DIRNAME = "goals"`, `RUN_FILENAME = "run.json"`,
  `LOCKS_DIRNAME = ".locks"`, `RUN_LOCKS_DIRNAME = "runs"`.
- src/goal/run-store.ts:21-28 `resolveGoalsDir(stateDir)` / `resolveRunDir(runId, goalsDir)` —
  state-dir aware so MOLTBOT_STATE_DIR is honoured (already verified in claim 1 by writing
  a run entirely under RELEASE_AUDIT/README_VERIFY/_state/goals/<runId>/).
- src/goal/run-store.ts:31-40 `atomicWriteJson` — temp + rename + chmod 0o600 (durable writes).
- src/goal/run-store.ts:43-47 `saveRun(run)` — every run mutation flushes a complete
  SerializedRun to disk.
- src/goal/run-store.ts:50-63 `loadRun(runId)` — reads run.json, applies migration via
  `migrateRun`, and rewrites the file on disk if the `state` field changed (so the on-disk
  representation converges to the current schema on every read).

Live-run lock tracking (the signal that distinguishes "actively running" from "crashed"):
- src/goal/run-store.ts:182-202 `hasActiveRunLock(runId, goalsDir)` — reads
  `<goalsDir>/.locks/runs/<runId>.lock`, parses `{pid}`, returns true iff `process.kill(pid, 0)`
  succeeds (run-store.ts:172-180 `isAlive`). On stale-lock detection it best-effort unlinks
  the lock file so the next caller treats the run as not-running.

Migration / reconciliation logic (the "stale work" recovery):
- src/goal/run-store.ts:66-170 `function migrateRun(data, goalsDir)`:
  * run-store.ts:88 `const keepInProgress = runId ? hasActiveRunLock(runId, goalsDir) : false;`
    — the lock decides whether legacy `running`/`in_progress` steps stay in progress or get
    rolled back to `pending` for replay.
  * run-store.ts:91-99 legacy `running` and `in_progress` step statuses are reset to
    `pending` when no live lock exists (process-crash mid-task recovery).
  * run-store.ts:100-111 legacy `failed`/`skipped` step statuses are converted to `blocked`
    with a synthesized `blockedReason`/`blockedQuestion` so the resume path has structured
    state to act on.
  * run-store.ts:131-151 the run-level `executing` state is recovered: if no live lock,
    `allStepsDone` flips it to `done`, otherwise it becomes `blocked` with a synthesized
    `BlockedDetail { blockedAt: "execution", prompt: "Run was interrupted (gateway restart
    or process exit). Use goal resume to continue.", requiredInputKey: "resume_execution" }`.
- src/goal/run-store.ts:223-247 `export function reconcileStaleRuns(goalsDir)` — sweeps the
  goals dir, force-loads every run whose on-disk `state === "executing"` (which triggers the
  migration above and rewrites the file), and returns the count of runs whose state changed.

Startup sweep (where reconciliation is wired):
- src/gateway/server-startup.ts:20 `import { reconcileStaleRuns } from "../goal/run-store.js";`
- src/gateway/server-startup.ts:43-50 the gateway startup sequence calls
  `reconcileStaleRuns()` and logs the number of recovered runs; failures are logged as a
  warning rather than aborting startup.

Resume entry point:
- src/commands/goal-resume.ts:13 `import { loadRun, ... }`
- src/commands/goal-resume.ts:340 `const run = loadRun(resolvedId);` — resume reads the
  reconciled run from disk (migrateRun runs as part of loadRun, so a crash-recovered run is
  already in the right shape when resume sees it).
- src/commands/goal-resume.ts:360 `if (run.state === "blocked") { ... }` — the resume path
  branches on the synthesized blocked state produced by reconciliation, which is how a run
  that crashed mid-execute becomes resumable.
- src/commands/goal-resume.ts:189 `const latestRun = loadRun(run.runId);` and
  goal-resume.ts:470 `const previousRun = loadRun(savedRunId);` — repeated reload-from-disk
  pattern keeps resume aligned with whatever migration / external mutation has happened.

== INTERPRETATION ==

- A run is fully recoverable from disk: every state transition writes the complete
  SerializedRun (atomic temp+rename), and `loadRun` rewrites the file whenever migration
  produces a different `state`, so the next reader sees a converged shape.
- Crash recovery is mechanical, not LLM-driven: a stale `executing` run is identified by the
  absence of a live process matching the recorded PID in `.locks/runs/<runId>.lock`, the run
  is force-rewritten as `blocked` with an `execution`-stage BlockedDetail (or `done` if all
  steps finished), and the gateway startup sequence performs this sweep automatically.
- Resume picks up from that reconciled state via `goal resume` reading the run.json from
  disk and branching on the migrated state.

== SAFE README WORDING ==

OK to say: "Runs persist to disk and survive process exits or gateway restarts; the next
gateway start reconciles any run that was executing without a live process and marks it
blocked so it can be resumed."
OK to say: "Resume reads the persisted run state from disk; nothing in-memory is required."

== UNSAFE WORDING TO AVOID ==

Avoid: "Crash-proof" or "exactly-once" — recovery rolls a partially-completed step back to
`pending` and replays it, which is at-least-once for the step the crash interrupted.
Avoid: "Live HA" or "hot failover" — reconciliation runs on gateway startup, not continuously.
Avoid: any claim that resume rolls back filesystem side effects produced by the worker
before the crash; only the run-state record is reconciled.

STATUS: verified
REASON: Atomic on-disk persistence (src/goal/run-store.ts:31-47), lock-aware migration
(src/goal/run-store.ts:66-151), the `reconcileStaleRuns` sweep
(src/goal/run-store.ts:223-247) wired into gateway startup
(src/gateway/server-startup.ts:43-50), and the resume command reading reconciled state via
`loadRun` (src/commands/goal-resume.ts:340, 360) together implement reconciliation of stale
in-progress work and resume-from-persisted-state.
